{"name":"Parlour Taupe","hex":"#d4c6af","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"parlour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Commonwealth standard spelling of parlor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/parlour"},{"term":"taupe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dark brownish-grey colour, the colour of moleskin."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark brownish-grey colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/taupe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Parlour","blocks":[{"text":"A parlour is a reception room or public space. In medieval Christian Europe, the \"outer parlour\" was the room where the monks or nuns conducted business with those outside the monastery and the \"inner parlour\" was used for necessary conversation between resident members. In the English-speaking world of the 18th and 19th century, having a parlour room was evidence of social status."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Ca%27_Rezzonico_-_Il_Parlatorio_delle_monache_di_San_Zaccaria_-_Francesco_Guardi.jpg/330px-Ca%27_Rezzonico_-_Il_Parlatorio_delle_monache_di_San_Zaccaria_-_Francesco_Guardi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parlour","wikidata":"Q10550521"},{"title":"Taupe","blocks":[{"text":"Taupe is a dark gray-brown color. The word derives from the French noun taupe meaning \"mole\". The name originally referred only to the average color of the French mole, but beginning in the 1940s, its usage expanded to encompass a wider range of shades."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taupe","wikidata":"Q2307593"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Photograph_of_a_Greek_Revival_Parlor_in_the_Metropolitan.jpg/500px-Photograph_of_a_Greek_Revival_Parlor_in_the_Metropolitan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip